Flagged in review as a structural risk, not a bug: README (e15a92a) had copied SKILL.md's full note-writing criteria, the flock/per-torrent-lock example, the good/bad note text, and the git notes add/append commands nearly verbatim. Two independently-maintained copies of the same content is exactly the mechanism behind a bug already paid for once -- the regex-escaping fix (52edaea) changed real behavior and both docs had to be caught and fixed separately because they'd drifted independently.

Chose SKILL.md as the canonical source, not README, because SKILL.md is what Claude actually loads and follows -- README describing the same rules independently is documentation of documentation, with no mechanism keeping them in sync. README now states the criteria exist and where to find them (one link) instead of repeating them.

Kept the per-script Usage entries and the push/fetch caveat in README rather than also delegating those to SKILL.md: those aren't duplicated anywhere (SKILL.md's own script descriptions serve a different purpose -- guiding Claude's choice of which script to run, not documenting invocation syntax for a human reading the repo on GitHub), and the push/fetch caveat is operationally critical enough that it was an explicit, deliberate requirement from this repo's very first setup (get a plain "why would notes not show up" question answered without needing to already know about SKILL.md).
